// @error share a field-name set and no literal-typed field distinguishes them
// A union whose two variants carry the SAME field names with DIFFERENT field types
// (`{v: i32} | {v: string}`). VL's structural unions discriminate on the field-NAME set —
// `variantSig`, the tag key, IS that set — so two such variants rank identically and every
// `is` test on either answers for both. Worse, the literal → variant resolution matches by
// field-name set alone, so the second member's literal builds the FIRST member's struct:
// this program emitted a module whose global initializer pushed a string-array ref into an
// i32 field, and both `is` tests compared against tag 0. Silent INVALID WASM, with no
// generics anywhere in it.
//
// There is no sound representation to give this union under name-set discrimination, so it
// is a loud reject naming both offending arms. Layout-EQUAL same-name variants (one shape
// reached through two spellings) stay legal — they are one variant, and the twin machinery
// already folds them; `union-variant-twin-*.vl` pin that side.
//
// THE STAGE MOVED, THE VERDICT DID NOT (D1023). This was an `@emit-error`, and an emit reject
// firing on a `vl check`-clean program is a clause-2 violation by construction: check returned
// 0 to reach the emitter, so either the program is legal or the CHECKER owed the diagnosis.
// Discriminating on the field-NAME set is a DESIGN rule (`docs/guide/unions.md`), so the
// checker owes it, and `assignTags` keeps its reject as an unreachable floor. The sibling
// shape the same ruling made LEGAL — arms parted by a literal-typed field — is
// `same-field-names-literal-tags.vl`; here neither field is a literal, so nothing tells the
// two arms apart and the refusal stands.
type U = { v: i32 } | { v: string }
const a: U = { v: 7 }
const b: U = { v: "s" }
if a is { v: i32 } { print(a.v) }
if b is { v: string } { print(b.v) }
